26.3  Log rotation and logs that fill the disk

TL;DR
  - journald limits itself: by default up to 10 % of its filesystem, at most 4
    GiB. SystemMaxUse= changes it.
  - logrotate handles text logs: on a schedule it renames the file, starts a new
    one, compresses old ones and deletes the oldest.
  - A log that grows without limit usually has no rotation rule, or a program
    writing too much.

      journalctl --disk-usage
      sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=500M      # shrink the journal now
      ⚠ changes or deletes data: check what it targets first
      sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf   # dry run: what would rotate

On your machine
  Journal   on disk (/var/log/journal): kept across reboots · 300 B in 2 files
  Settings  SystemMaxUse=500M
  Syslog    rsyslog also writes text files in /var/log
  Rotation  2 rules in /etc/logrotate.d
  Biggest   /var/log/syslog 4.9 KiB · /var/log/nginx/access.log 2.9 KiB ·
            /var/log/auth.log 1.2 KiB

Pitfalls
  - Deleting a big log a program still writes to frees nothing: the file stays
    open. Empty it instead (: > file), or restart the program; sudo lsof +L1
    lists deleted files still held open.
  - After rotation, a program writing to the renamed file must be told to reopen
    (postrotate with a reload), or rotation must use copytruncate.
  - Docker's default log driver keeps container output forever: set max-size and
    max-file in /etc/docker/daemon.json.

Deep dive

  ▾ What does a logrotate rule look like?

        /var/log/myapp/*.log {
            weekly
            rotate 4
            compress
            missingok
            postrotate
                systemctl reload myapp
            endscript
        }

    Four weeks are kept, compressed, then deleted. A systemd timer (or cron)
    runs logrotate daily; each rule decides how often it acts.

  ▾ Why do journald limits sometimes not apply?

    They count only the journal's own files, archived ones first: the file being
    written stays until it's rotated. Limits are checked as it writes, so after
    lowering SystemMaxUse=, run --vacuum-size or restart journald to shrink at
    once.

Related
  Commands  journalctl, logrotate, lsof
  Paths     /etc/logrotate.conf, /etc/logrotate.d/, /etc/systemd/journald.conf
  Hub       atfq logs Logs
  See also  atfq 26.2 Where logs go: journald, syslog and /var/log
  See also  atfq 13.4 Links, inodes and "disk full"
